Command               	Purpose
ls	            List files and directories
cd	            Change the current directory
pwd           	Print the current working directory
cat           	Display or combine file contents
echo	          Print text or variables
man	            Display a command's manual page
history	        Show previously executed commands
clear	          Clear the terminal screen

ls — List Directory Contents...Displays the files and folders in the current directory.
Syntax: ls [options] [directory]
 Flag     Description              
-l       Long listing format (permissions, owner, size, date) 
-a   Show hidden files (those beginning with `.`)         
-h   Human-readable file sizes (used with `-l`)           
-R   Recursively list subdirectories                      
-t   Sort by modification time                            
-S   Sort by file size                                    

cd — Change Directory....Moves between directories.
Syntax: cd directory
Command    	Meaning
cd	      Go to home directory
cd ..   	Move up one directory
cd -    	Return to previous directory
cd ~    	Go to home directory
cd /    	Go to filesystem root

pwd — Print Working Directory....Displays the full path of your current location.
Syntax: pwd
Flag  	 Description
-P	   Show the physical path (resolve symbolic links)

cat — Concatenate and Display Files....Displays the contents of files or combines multiple files.
Syntax: cat [options] file
Flag        	Description
-n	      Number all output lines
-b	      Number non-empty lines only
-E	      Show $ at the end of each line    #use-case: Detecting Trailing Whitespace (Spaces/Tabs)
-T      	Display tabs as ^I               #use-case: Debugging Python/YAML Indentation Errors i.e differentiate good indentation tab from space error

echo — Print Text...Outputs text or variable values.
Syntax: echo [options] text
Flag	    Description
-n	    Don't print a newline
-e    	Enable interpretation of escape sequences

man — Manual Pages....Shows the built-in documentation for commands.
Syntax: man command
Flag   	Description
-k 	   Search manual pages by keyword  #man -k copy
-f   	Show a short description (equivalent to whatis) #man -f ls
Navigation - Inside man:
Key       	Action
Space    	Next page
b	        Previous page
/         text	Search
n        	Next search result
q        	Quit

history — Command History....Shows commands you've previously executed.
Syntax: history
Option	          Description
history N    	Show the last N commands his
history -c   	Clear the current shell's history list
history -d    OFFSET	Delete the history entry at a given position

Note: history -c clears the current shell's in-memory history. Depending on your shell configuration, the history file on disk may also need to be updated
(for example, by exiting the shell) For the change to persist.
TO WORK WITH HISTORY
!45 #Run command number 45 again
!! #Run the previous command
!git #Repeat the last command that started with git

Command       	Purpose
cp	     Copy files and directories
mv     	Move or rename files and directories
rm     	Remove files and directories
mkdir  	Create directories
rmdir  	Remove empty directories
touch	  Create empty files or update timestamps

cp — Copy Files and Directories...Copies one or more files or directories to another location.
Syntax: cp [options] source destination
NOTE: using cp to a directory, should be cp t.txt directory/ #tells Linux, this is a directory, if it's missing, it returns an error
#without /, if the directory doesn't exist, it copies & creates a new file in the current directory. or it occupies another file with the same name

Flag         	Description
-r, -R	   Copy directories recursively #with links, it copies destination instead of the actual link as a link.
-i       	Prompt before overwriting files #are 
-f      	 Force overwrite without prompting
-u      	 Copy only if the source is newer than the destination
-v	       Verbose mode (show files being copied)
-p      	 Preserve permissions, ownership, and timestamps
-a       	Archive mode (preserves nearly everything; recursive) #this is a combo of -a and -p, it also copies links as links, requires sudo to cp files of others
-n       	Do not overwrite existing files

mv — Move or Rename Files...Moves files/directories or renames them.
Syntax: mv [options] source destination eg. mv -v *.txt Documents/ - moves all .txt files 
Flag      	Description
-i	      Ask before overwriting
-f	      Force overwrite
-n	      Never overwrite existing files
-u      	Move only if the source is newer
-v      	Show what is being moved

rm — Remove Files....Deletes files and directories.
Warning: Deleted files generally cannot be recovered using rm.
Syntax: rm [options] file
flag	            Description
-r, -R    	Remove directories recursively
-f        	Force deletion without prompts
-i        	Prompt before each removal
-I        	Prompt once before removing many files or recursively
-v	        Show each file removed
-d        	Remove an empty directory

mkdir — Make Directories....Creates one or more directories.
Syntax: mkdir [options] directory
NOTE: mkdir Docs Images Videos # makes multiple directories ... 
Flag           	Description
-p         	Create parent directories as needed #mkdir -p Projects/Python/Week1 # creates nested directories
-v         	Show each directory created
-m MODE    	Set permissions when creating the directory #mkdir -m 755 website

rmdir — Remove Empty Directories....Deletes directories only if they are empty.
Syntax: rmdir [options] directory
Flag                                     	Description
-p                             	Remove parent directories if they become empty #useful for nested directories
-v	                             Display each directory removed
--ignore-fail-on-non-empty	     Ignore errors if a directory isn't empty (GNU implementation)

touch — Create Files or Update Timestamps....Creates an empty file if it doesn't exist, or updates the file's timestamps if it does.
Syntax: touch [options] file
Flag    	Description
-a   	Update only the access time
-m   	Update only the modification time
-c	   Don't create the file if it doesn't exist
-t    TIME	Set a specific timestamp #touch -t 202607051230 report.txt (Format: [[CC]YY]MMDDhhmm[.ss]) [] denotes optional field, ie., century, year, seconds
-r    FILE	Copy timestamps from another file #touch -r original.txt copy.txt

NOTE: the difference between touch, echo, and cat: creating (touch), writing (echo), and reading (cat).
NOTE: Every file on Linux/Unix has three different timestamps (clocks) attached to it:
- Access time (atime) – The last time the file was read (opened, viewed, or accessed).
- Modify time (mtime) – The last time the contents of the file were changed (written to).
- Change time (ctime) – The last time the file's metadata (permissions, ownership, or filename) was changed. 
       ..(Note: You cannot manually set ctime with touch; the system updates it automatically.)

less -  Pager like vim/nano to read the contents of a large text file one screenful (one page) at a time, instead of dumping everything onto your terminal at once.
Flag          	What it does                         	                Example
-N          	Show line numbers	                                    less -N /var/log/syslog
-i    	    Case-insensitive search                                	less -i logfile.log then search /error
-s   	    Squeeze multiple blank lines into one	                   less -s bigfile.txt
-S	    Chop long lines (no wrapping); side-scroll with arrows	     less -S widefile.log
-m    	More detailed prompt (file position, %)                    	less -m file.txt
-x3	    Set tab width to 3 spaces (good for code)                	 less -x3 source.c
-X	    Leave file contents on screen after exiting	                less -X file.txt
-F	    Exit automatically if file fits on one screen              	less -F short.conf
+F	    Follow mode (like tail -f, but navigable)                  	less +F /var/log/nginx/access.log
+G    	Start at the end of the file                               	less +G /var/log/syslog
+100    	Start at line 100	                                        less +100 bigfile.txt
-p     PATTERN	Start at first match of PATTERN                    	less -p "ERROR" app.log

for navigation in less: 
Key                     	What it does
Spacebar or f	    Move forward (down) one full page.
b	                Move backward (up) one full page.
Enter	            Move forward one single line at a time (down).
y                	Move backward one single line at a time (up).
g                	Go to the very top of the file.
G (Shift + g)	    Go to the very bottom of the file.
/ + searchterm	   Search forward for a word. (Press n to go to the next match, N for the previous).
? + searchterm   	Search backward for a word.
q	                Quit and exit less (return to your normal terminal prompt).
Pro-Tips for less
- Read while following (tail -f style): If you press Shift + F while inside less, it will enter "follow mode"—it will keep showing you new lines 
as they are added to the file (just like tail -f). Press Ctrl + C to stop following and go back to normal navigation.

- View multiple files: You can pass multiple files to less: less file1.txt file2.txt
Type :n to go to the next file, and :p to go to the previous file.

- Show line numbers: Start less with the -N flag to display line numbers on the left side:
less -N myscript.py

NOTE: There is a more command but, less is better than more

-head & tail
head shows you the top (beginning) of a file.
tail shows you the bottom (end) of a file.

syntax: head [options] filename
Flag	            Description                                                                          	Example
-n N	         Print the first N lines.	                                                      head -n 20 file.txt (shows first 20 lines)
-c N	         Print the first N bytes (characters) of the file.	                             head -c 100 file.txt (shows first 100 characters)
-q	           Quiet mode. When viewing multiple files, suppress the filename headers.       	head -q file1.txt file2.txt
-v           	Always show the filename header (useful when viewing multiple files).         	head -v *.txt

syntax: tail [options] filename
Flag	                Description                                                                                                   	Example
-n N	           Print the last N lines.                                                                             	tail -n 50 file.txt (shows last 50 lines)
-c N           	Print the last N bytes of the file.                                                                	tail -c 1000 file.txt (last 1000 characters)
-f             	Follow the file. This is the superstar flag! It prints the last 10 lines and then keeps watching the file.    	tail -f logfile.log
                 As new lines are added, they stream live to your terminal.
-F	             Follow with retry. Same as -f, but if the file is deleted or renamed (common in log rotation),                 tail -F /var/log/syslog
                 tail -F will keep trying to reopen it.	
-n +N	          Start printing from line N to the end of the file. (Notice the + sign).	                           tail -n +100 file.txt (prints from line 100 to the end)
--pid=PID	       Used with -f. Automatically stop following the file when the process with the given PID (Process ID) dies.	    tail -f file.log --pid=1234


ln
in Linux/Unix, there are two completely different types of links: Hard Links and Symbolic (Soft) Links.
Understanding the difference is crucial because they behave very differently when you delete, move, or edit the original file.
- A Hard Link is an exact mirror (an extra name for the exact same data on the hard drive).
- A Symbolic Link is a pointer (a shortcut that points to the original filename).

Hard Links (The "Twin"）
A hard link creates a second directory entry that points directly to the same physical data (inodes) on your hard drive.
ln hard link is different from cp on ln; it consumes little extra space, editing either file changes the other, and cp is independent after creation.
 syntax: ln original.txt hardlink.txt
The Rules of Hard Links:
- They share the same data: If you change original.txt, hardlink.txt changes too (and vice versa).
- They are equal: There is no "original" vs "link". Both are just names for the same file.
- Deleting doesn't hurt: If you delete original.txt, hardlink.txt still contains all the data. 
  The data only gets deleted from the hard drive when ALL hard links to it are deleted.
- Limitations: You cannot create a hard link for a directory (to avoid infinite loops). 
  You cannot create a hard link across different hard drives or partitions (because each drive has its own inode table).

Symbolic (Soft) Links (The "Shortcut")
A symbolic link is a special file that stores the path to the original file. It is exactly like a shortcut in Windows or an alias on Mac.
Syntax: ln -s original.txt softlink.txt # (note the -s flag):
The Rules of Symbolic Links:
- They are separate files: The link has its own inode and just holds the text of the file path (e.g., "/home/user/original.txt"). It takes almost zero disk space.
- They break easily: If you delete, rename, or move original.txt, the symbolic link breaks and becomes a "dangling link" 
    (it will flash red in ls and give a "No such file or directory" error).
- They are flexible: You can create symbolic links across different hard drives, partitions, and even to directories.

NOTE:
If you do ln -s folder1 folder2, you are creating a symbolic link.
BUT—if you accidentally do ln -s /home/user/myfile.txt mylink and then try to edit mylink with a text editor,
you are actually editing the original file. However, if you do rm mylink, you are only deleting the link, not the original.

The golden rule:
rm on a symlink = removes just the pointer (safe).
rm on a hard link = removes just that name (safe, data remains).
rm on the last remaining hard link = permanently deletes the data from the disk.

Goal	Command
ln -s /path/to/original /path/to/link #Create a symbolic link to a file	
ln -s /path/to/folder /path/to/link #Create a symbolic link to a folder	
ln /path/to/original /path/to/link #Create a hard link
ln -sf new_target old_link #Add the -f (force) flag: ln -sf new_target old_link

Check what a link points to	ls -l (it will show link -> target) or readlink link_name
